# OpenCPN External Control Demo — @PLATFORM@

This isolated developer package demonstrates the authenticated OpenCPN
`/api/v2` external-control architecture with xGRIB, chart-aware
xWeatherRouting, the modified o-charts semantic provider, the upgraded
Climatology 2.0.3 with dataset 2026.2, Polar, Celestial Navigation 2.9.12 and the
human-readable Scheduler. It targets `@EXPECTED_UNAME@` systems running
the `@PLATFORM@` build environment; native packages should not be treated as
portable across incompatible distributions.

This 25 November refresh includes xWeatherRouting 1.44.3, xGRIB 0.4.6 and
Generator 0.4.0, and updates the modified o-charts provider and its official
licensed helper/runtime to 2.2.5. The core fixes double daylight-saving-time
adjustment on modern wxWidgets and carries the bounded GSHHS corrections from
[OpenCPN PR 5535](https://github.com/OpenCPN/OpenCPN/pull/5535): crossing
answers are independent of endpoint order, and disjoint collinear segments
are no longer reported as intersections. The existing absolute tolerances
are retained; further tolerance changes remain separate work.

The shoreline installer creates its data directory before downloading High or
Full GSHHG. The plug-in supplies Crude, Low and Intermediate for offline
installation. Cumulative Map and Cumulative - Calms use the legacy isochrone
calculation and show a warning when this changes the selected routing method.

Route diagnostics report the requested UTC time and, when the reader supplies
it, the end of the usable wind forecast. The xGRIB 0.4.6 preview build provides
that end time for missing wind responses. On a long passage, Climatology can
supply wind after the forecast ends when fallback is enabled.

Meridian is a new independent routing method with a full-circle heading fan,
coastal step refinement and geographic detour guidance. Select Meridian alone,
or tick it separately in Auto/All; it runs after Atlas and starts unticked.
The Donegal fix guides medium coastal passages to their actual destination.
The established search implementations and saved engine selections are retained.
All methods apply the full requested shoreline buffer while keeping bounded
water-endpoint access. Live diagnostics distinguish a completed method from
other methods still running in All. These are bounded searches: no feasible
route or fastest-passage guarantee is implied by a failed or completed attempt.

xWeatherRouting includes opt-in additional calculation helpers in
Settings → Computation, off on a fresh installation and remembered thereafter.
Parallel routing methods retain their separate saved setting. The core supports
verified global open-water starting layers, bounded isolated detailed-tile
helpers and reuse of chart-safe data, with authoritative final validation.
Helpers require Python 3 and Xvfb; unavailable helpers use the ordinary chart
path. Import your own compatible verified starting layer; this clean bundle
does not include personal chart caches or chart data.

The retained 1.42.0 adapter optimisation avoids copying unused boat-polar tables for each weather or
land query; boat-performance calculations retain the full polar data. This
reduces allocation overhead without changing the search engines or safety
rules. Endpoint preflight now runs before every routing mode, including Auto
and All. Confirmed land endpoints stop with their name, coordinates and data
source. Water endpoints within the shore margin show a Continue/Cancel warning
and retain the existing bounded coastal-access policy. Unavailable chart
evidence remains distinct from confirmed land. Settings help describes these
checks and the optional computation controls.

Routings → Chart Awareness Settings shows whether a starting layer is
installed and active for the loaded charts. It shows geographic bounds,
verified regions and regions using detailed checks. Newly imported layers
preserve their name and release version when supplied.

With persistent chart caching on, the preview launcher enables reuse of failed
CM93 open-water proof attempts across restarts for the same chart catalogue
and source files. A cache hit still requires detailed chart checks. This
reduces repeated proof work in the saved Atlantic replay. Full-passage timing
remains under evaluation. Use
`OCPN_CHART_SAFETY_REUSE_DETAILED_REGIONS=0` to compare with reuse disabled.

The core fixes persistent certificate round trips: integral JSON numbers are
converted explicitly, invalid/stale proofs are rejected, and derived format-4
certificates bind to the chart catalogue. Older certificates rebuild on demand;
raw semantic tile dependencies and global-layer validation remain enforced.
CM93 loading uses temporary append cursors while preserving natural object
order. Controlled local chart-loading replay improved from 287 to 119 seconds
with byte-identical safety tiles. This is a component measurement, not a general
whole-passage speed claim. No routing engine or safety rule was changed by
these two core fixes.

Automatic adaptive numerical settings and exact saved manual values remain
available. Wind, waves, boat performance and chart/depth requirements continue
to determine which routes can be accepted.

xWeatherRouting identifies coincident consecutive waypoints before creating
or computing legs, naming the points and leg number. Nearby distinct points
and legitimate return passages remain accepted. Its independent shoreline
provider and chart/depth checks are retained. xGRIB 0.4.6 natively decodes CCSDS-compressed GRIB2 forecasts (template 42),
including the ECMWF files that previously needed repacking. It displays
externally generated forecasts and retains early
Copernicus NWS time-range validation and the preview environmental adapter.
Climatology 2.0.3 includes corrected, indexed historical cyclone queries and
immutable filtered indexes; routing warnings remain explicit about fallback use.

The earlier Dateline correction is retained. CM93 coverage selection and
safety-grid traversal stay local at ±180°. Its existing cache identity
continues to separate older tiles and certificates. Land, drying,
shallow-water and genuinely unknown-depth checks remain active.

The Routing weather preset now includes gusts where the selected provider
supplies them. Existing Routing GRIB files need regeneration. Recompute routes
with the new forecast to populate the gust column. Gust speeds display to one
decimal in the selected units; calculations retain full precision. AIFS does
not supply gusts. Hosted GFS gust-aware fallback requires service 0.2.2 and a
five-field weather cache; an older service fails explicitly rather than
returning a successful gust-free forecast.

The launcher retains the qualified whole-tile proof path. A tile is certified
only with complete chart coverage and conservative area-depth evidence;
isolated hazards and missing evidence retain detailed checks. Coastal endpoint
access remains bounded, with full clearance offshore. The performance guides
record historical core evidence; the chart-loading replay above is separate from bundle-wide performance qualification. Exact revisions
are in `COMPONENTS.md`.

Celestial Navigation 2.9.12 includes corrected CSV index-error units and the
current offline almanac, planner, lunar and compact-ephemeris tools. The
previously qualified Debian future-UTC and lunar-fixture compatibility fixes
are retained on the pinned preview branch.

This bundle retains the qualified external-control preview host and native
API 1.21 plugins. The separate modular HostApi123 proposal and its API 1.22
review consumer are described in [OpenCPN PR 5475](https://github.com/OpenCPN/OpenCPN/pull/5475).
They are not substituted into this bundle.

It is a developer demonstration, not a navigation release. It does not replace
an existing OpenCPN installation, use an existing profile, activate routes or
send autopilot output. Chart, credential and forecast data are not included.

Install the target-distribution runtime prerequisites first. For Debian 12:

```sh
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install curl file iproute2 libaec0 libarchive13 libblosc1 \
  libeccodes0 libgdk-pixbuf2.0-bin libglew2.2 libglu1-mesa libhdf5-103-1 \
  libhdf5-hl-100 libnetcdf19 libportaudio2 libproj25 libqhull-r8.0 \
  librsvg2-common libshp4 libsodium23 libsz2 libtinyxml2.6.2v5 libunarr1 \
  libusb-1.0-0 libwxgtk-gl3.2-1 libwxgtk-webview3.2-1 libzip4 \
  python3-tk python3-venv xauth xvfb
```

For Debian 13 x86_64:

```sh
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install curl file iproute2 libaec0 libarchive13t64 libblosc1 \
  libeccodes0 libgdk-pixbuf2.0-bin libglew2.2 libglu1-mesa libhdf5-310 \
  libhdf5-hl-310 libnetcdf22 libportaudio2 libproj25 libqhull-r8.0 \
  librsvg2-common libshp4 libsodium23 libsz2 libtinyxml2.6.2v5 libusb-1.0-0 \
  libwxgtk-gl3.2-1t64 libwxgtk-webview3.2-1t64 libzip5 \
  python3-tk python3-venv xauth xvfb
```

The demo installer runs as your normal user. These are normal host runtime
packages used by OpenCPN and the bundled environmental-data helper.

Install into a new directory:

```sh
sha256sum --check ../OpenCPN-External-Control-Demo-*.tar.gz.sha256
./install-external-control-demo.sh "$PWD/Test-OpenCPN-External-Control-Demo"
Test-OpenCPN-External-Control-Demo/bin/launch-opencpn-external-control-demo
```

The installer checks the CPU architecture and every embedded checksum before
extracting. It stages the complete installation, reports missing shared
libraries, refuses to overwrite a non-empty target and generates a fresh
owner-only least-privilege token.

The native plug-ins are part of this package and are visible in **Options →
Plugins** without selecting **Update Catalog Master**. xGRIB,
xWeatherRouting, o-charts, Climatology, Polar and Celestial Navigation are
initially enabled; bundled GRIB is present for compatibility but disabled to
avoid two GRIB displays competing.
The exact list is in `docs/PLUGIN-INVENTORY.md` after installation.

To update a plugin, use **Options → Plugins → Import plugin…** with a tarball
matching this bundle's Debian version and CPU architecture. When running in a
container, match the container's Debian version. Launch through the supplied
launcher: imports replace the copy in this writable preview installation and
their installation records stay in its private profile. Restart OpenCPN after
updating an active plugin. Do not add your normal `~/.local/lib/opencpn`
directory to the launcher's search path.

The Python SDK, `opencpnctl`, least-privilege MCP adapter and Scheduler are
installed in the isolated `client` environment. MCP hosts should launch
`bin/launch-opencpn-mcp-external-control-demo`; it supplies only the demo's
loopback URL and owner-only token. The qualified surface is recorded in
`docs/FULL-SYSTEM-CHECKLIST.md`.

The isolated profile explicitly permits these pinned, audited plug-ins from
the bundle's private system-plugin directory. This does not alter the host's
OpenCPN profile or relax the policy for any other installation.

Celestial Navigation's large eclipse kernels and optional lunar-terrain pack
are not bundled or downloaded automatically. They are unnecessary for its
almanac, sight-planning and fix features. Developers who want the complete
offline eclipse feature should follow the pinned source and data links in
`docs/CELESTIAL-ECLIPSE-DATA.md`.

The bundle contains no licensed chart or entitlement data. On first start, add
only charts which are legitimately licensed to the test machine. The modified
o-charts provider does not export plaintext charts; it supplies derived
land/depth semantics to the hardened core in bounded batches. Read
`docs/CHART-AWARE-ROUTING-PREVIEW.md` before testing xWeatherRouting; it
describes fallback, pre-warm, on-demand expansion, cache and reporting
behaviour. Close OpenCPN cleanly before first launching the Scheduler:

```sh
Test-OpenCPN-External-Control-Demo/bin/launch-opencpn-scheduler-external-control-demo
```

Please report the package platform, Raspberry Pi model or computer, operating
system, wxWidgets version, the redacted OpenCPN log and the exact workflow
which failed. Never publish API tokens, provider passwords or licensed charts.
